"""
Retrieve Animation films released from 2017 through 2019 with a rental rate greater than 1, sorted alphabetically.

Here I am using INNER JOIN because we only want films that have a matching category.

I would choose the direct JOIN
method because it is more readable and easier to understand for this task. 
A CTE is not really needed because we do not have repeated logic, and a subquery would make the solution more complex without 
giving us a clear advantage.

Additionally, we can compare the execution time of each query using EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS). 
There are other criteria that can be used to analyse query performance, such as planning time and buffer usage, 
but I decided to use execution time to keep the comparison simple and easy to understand.

Results:

JOIN: 0.572 ms
CTE: 0.486 ms
Subquery: 0.604 ms

The results show only a very small difference in execution time. Therefore, 
we can say that all three queries have similar performance in this case. In terms of readability, 
I would choose the JOIN method because it is the simplest and easiest to understand.

All the queries are listed below.

""" --JOIN solution
--EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) to check the execution time 
SELECT f.title,
    f.rental_rate
FROM public.category AS c
    INNER JOIN public.film_category AS fc ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
    INNER JOIN public.film AS f ON f.film_id = fc.film_id
WHERE c.name = 'Animation'
    AND f.release_year BETWEEN 2017 AND 2019
    AND f.rental_rate > 1
ORDER BY f.title ASC;
-- CTE solution 
WITH animation_films AS (
    SELECT f.title,
        f.rental_rate
    FROM public.category AS c
        INNER JOIN public.film_category AS fc ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
        INNER JOIN public.film AS f ON f.film_id = fc.film_id
    WHERE c.name = 'Animation'
        AND f.release_year BETWEEN 2017 AND 2019
        AND f.rental_rate > 1
)
SELECT *
FROM animation_films
ORDER BY title ASC;
-- Subquery solution
SELECT animation_films.title,
    animation_films.rental_rate
FROM (
        SELECT f.title,
            f.rental_rate
        FROM public.category AS c
            INNER JOIN public.film_category AS fc ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
            INNER JOIN public.film AS f ON f.film_id = fc.film_id
        WHERE c.name = 'Animation'
            AND f.release_year BETWEEN 2017 AND 2019
            AND f.rental_rate > 1
    ) AS animation_films
ORDER BY animation_films.title ASC;
-- task 2
/*
 Task: Calculate the revenue earned by each rental store after March 2017, including the combined address and revenue.
 
 The query uses INNER JOIN because the report should include stores with matching address, inventory, rental and payment records.
 The relationship path is:
 
 store → address
 store → inventory
 inventory → rental
 rental → payment
 
 The revenue is calculated with SUM(payment.amount). The date condition is applied to payment.payment_date because revenue is recorded when the payment is made. The condition begins on 2017-04-01 because the task says “after March 2017.”
 
 The address and address2 fields are combined into one output column using CONCAT_WS. This function is useful because it handles NULL values in address2 without producing an unnecessary separator.
 
 For this task, I would use the direct JOIN solution in production because the query has one clear aggregation step and the table relationships are easy to understand. 
 The CTE version improves readability by separating the payment collection step from the aggregation step. 
 The subquery version is valid but introduces an additional nesting level, so it is less readable for this case.
 
 The results of query execution was almost the same therefor I decided not to include it to the report.
 
 */
-- Direct JOIN
SELECT s.store_id,
    CONCAT_WS(', ', a.address, a.address2) AS store_address,
    -- CONCAT_WS combines colums into a one
    SUM(p.amount) AS revenue
FROM public.store AS s
    INNER JOIN public.address AS a ON a.address_id = s.address_id
    INNER JOIN public.inventory AS i ON i.store_id = s.store_id
    INNER JOIN public.rental AS r ON r.inventory_id = i.inventory_id
    INNER JOIN public.payment AS p ON p.rental_id = r.rental_id
WHERE p.payment_date >= DATE '2017-04-01'
GROUP BY s.store_id,
    a.address,
    a.address2
ORDER BY s.store_id;
-- CTE
WITH store_revenue AS(
    SELECT s.store_id,
        CONCAT_WS(', ', a.address, a.address2) AS store_address,
        SUM(p.amount) AS revenue
    FROM public.store AS s
        INNER JOIN public.address AS a ON a.address_id = s.address_id
        INNER JOIN public.inventory AS i ON i.store_id = s.store_id
        INNER JOIN public.rental AS r ON r.inventory_id = i.inventory_id
        INNER JOIN public.payment AS p ON p.rental_id = r.rental_id
    WHERE p.payment_date >= DATE '2017-04-01'
    GROUP BY s.store_id,
        a.address,
        a.address2
)
SELECT *
FROM store_revenue
ORDER BY store_id ASC;
-- Subquery
SELECT sr.store_id,
    sr.store_address,
    SUM(sr.amount) AS revenue
FROM (
        SELECT s.store_id,
            CONCAT_WS(', ', a.address, a.address2) AS store_address,
            p.amount
        FROM public.store AS s
            INNER JOIN public.address AS a ON a.address_id = s.address_id
            INNER JOIN public.inventory AS i ON i.store_id = s.store_id
            INNER JOIN public.rental AS r ON r.inventory_id = i.inventory_id
            INNER JOIN public.payment AS p ON p.rental_id = r.rental_id
        WHERE p.payment_date >= DATE '2017-04-01'
    ) AS sr
GROUP BY sr.store_id,
    sr.store_address
ORDER BY sr.store_id ASC;
